<section id="section-0" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <GetYourGuideCity locationId="16" />
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">Why Visit Paris at Christmas?</h2>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>Okay, let’s be honest. Paris is beautiful any time of year, but Christmas gives the city an extra sparkle — perhaps even enough to make Scrooge forget about his famous “Bah, humbug!”</p>
    <p>The shorter winter days, the twinkling lights, the decorated streets and shop windows, and the cozy cafés all come together and make you experience Paris in a different way.</p>
    <p>And that’s probably my favorite thing about Christmas in Paris: you still get to experience all the things that make Paris, well, Paris — but with a little extra magic!</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-1" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">Best Things to Do in Paris at Christmas</h2>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">1. Visit a Christmas Market</h3>
    <ArticleImage src={thingsToDoHero} alt="A festive ferris wheel at a Christmas market in Paris" caption="A ferris wheel at one of Paris' Christmas markets" imgClass="aspect-[5/4] object-cover" />
    <p>One thing you should know before visiting Paris at Christmas is that there are a lot of Christmas markets to choose from. They’re spread across the city, and each one has its own atmosphere, location and character. But that doesn’t mean you need to visit them all to get into the Christmas spirit.</p>
    <p>I decided to visit just three during my trip, mainly because I wanted to combine them with the places I already had on my itinerary. I’ll tell you more about the three I chose, and why, further down in this guide.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">2. Experience Christmas at Galeries Lafayette</h3>
    <ArticleImage src={galeriesLafayette} alt="The giant Christmas tree under the glass dome at Galeries Lafayette in Paris" caption="The Christmas tree at Galeries Lafayette" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
    <p>Galeries Lafayette is a must during the festive season. One of the highlights is the giant Christmas tree underneath the beautiful glass dome, which is different every year. If you want to take a photo from the Glasswalk, you’ll need to book a time slot in advance on the official Galeries Lafayette website. Also, during the Christmas season, the rooftop is transformed into an ice skating rink. So, if you want to skate there, you’ll need to book a time slot and purchase a ticket in advance.</p>
    <p>And don’t leave Galeries Lafayette without trying a macaron from Pierre Hermé. They were honestly the best macarons I’ve ever tasted — and believe me, I’ve tried a lot of macarons in Paris.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">3. See Paris’ Christmas Lights After Dark</h3>
    <ArticleImage src={champsElysees} alt="The Champs-Élysées lit up with Christmas lights in Paris" caption="Christmas lights on the Champs-Élysées" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    <p>Paris is at its prettiest once the Christmas lights come on. Head to places like the Champs-Élysées, Place Vendôme and Avenue Montaigne to see some of the city’s most beautiful festive decorations and enjoy Paris after dark.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">4. Take a Festive Seine Cruise</h3>
    <p>If you want to see Paris from a different perspective, a festive Seine cruise is a lovely option, especially in the evening when the city’s landmarks are beautifully lit up. You can choose from different dinner and sightseeing cruises, depending on what kind of experience you’re looking for.</p>
    <GetYourGuideAvailability
      tourId="405257"
      currency="EUR"
      variant="horizontal"
      poweredByHref="https://www.getyourguide.com/paris-l16/"
    />
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">5. Spend a Day at Disneyland Paris</h3>
    <p>If you’re traveling with kids or simply a Disney fan, you can add a little extra Christmas magic to your trip by spending a day at Disneyland Paris. During the festive season, known as Disney Enchanted Christmas, the parks are filled with festive decorations, shows, Disney characters and Christmas celebrations. Keep in mind that Disneyland Paris is located outside the city, so it’s best to plan it as a full-day trip from Paris. It’s easily accessible by RER A, with the journey taking around 35–40 minutes.</p>
    <GetYourGuideAvailability
      tourId="395320"
      currency="EUR"
      variant="horizontal"
      poweredByHref="https://www.getyourguide.com/paris-l16/"
    />
  </div>
</section>

<section id="section-2" class="scroll-mt-nav">
  <div class="mb-5 max-w-3xl text-black md:mb-6">
    <h2 class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-semibold text-neutral-900 mb-4">Best Christmas Markets in Paris</h2>
  </div>
  <div class={prose}>
    <p>One thing that I found surprisingly difficult when planning my Christmas trip to Paris was deciding which Christmas markets were actually worth visiting. Paris doesn’t have just one or two — there are so many different markets around the city, from small local ones to huge Christmas villages like the one at La Défense. In the end, I decided to visit only three, mainly because I wanted to combine them with places I had loved during my first trip to Paris, including Notre-Dame and Montmartre.</p>
    <p>So, here are the three Christmas markets I visited in Paris and would recommend adding to your itinerary.</p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">1. Tuileries Christmas Market</h3>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 md:gap-4">
      <ArticleImage src={tuileriesFerrisWheel} alt="The Ferris wheel and Christmas pyramid at the Tuileries Christmas Market" caption="The Tuileries Christmas Market at dusk" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={tuileriesFood} alt="A hot dog and cheese fries from a food stall at the Tuileries Christmas Market" caption="Festive market food at the Tuileries" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>The Tuileries Christmas Market was the one I liked the most, and I actually visited it twice during my trip. It’s one of the largest Christmas markets in central Paris, with plenty of food, drinks, Christmas stalls and festive entertainment. The food was good and it had pretty much everything you would expect from a traditional Christmas market.</p>
    <p>One thing to keep in mind, though: it gets busier and busier as the day goes on, so if you prefer a more relaxed experience, I’d recommend going earlier.</p>
    <p><em>2026 dates: November 15 – December 31</em></p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">2. Notre-Dame Christmas Market</h3>
    <ArticleImage src={notreDameMarket} alt="A Christmas market stall in front of Notre-Dame Cathedral in Paris" caption="The Christmas market by Notre-Dame" imgClass="aspect-[2/3] object-cover" />
    <p>The Notre-Dame Christmas Market is much smaller and has a more intimate feel. You’ll find French crafts, local creators and regional food, so it’s a nice place to stop for a little browsing while exploring the area.</p>
    <p>I mainly visited this one because I wanted to see Notre-Dame again. After the cathedral reopened following its restoration, I simply couldn’t visit Paris without going inside — and combining it with a little Christmas market stop made it even better.</p>
    <p><em>2026 dates: November 26 – December 27</em></p>
    <h3 class="mt-8 mb-2 text-black">3. Montmartre Christmas Market</h3>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-3 md:gap-4">
      <ArticleImage src={montmartreMetro} alt="The Art Nouveau Metropolitain sign near the Montmartre Christmas Market" caption="Near Abbesses, by the Montmartre Christmas Market" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
      <ArticleImage src={montmartreCarousel} alt="A carousel at the Montmartre Christmas Market" caption="The carousel at the Montmartre Christmas Market" imgClass="aspect-[4/5] object-cover" />
    </div>
    <p>The Montmartre Christmas Market is located right by Place des Abbesses, just outside the metro station, so you can’t really miss it when you arrive. It’s a much smaller market, but it’s a nice little stop before exploring Montmartre.</p>
    <p>I visited in the morning, and one of my favorite little moments was grabbing a fresh baguette for breakfast before continuing my walk around Montmartre. Honestly, that’s exactly the kind of simple Parisian moment I was hoping for.</p>
  </div>
</section>

